<div class="tldr"><p><strong>Short answer:</strong> bibs are ski or snowboard pants with a built-in chest panel and shoulder straps. They keep snow out of your waistband and add a little warmth, which makes them great for powder, park, beginners who fall a lot, and kids. Regular pants are simpler, cooler on warm days, and easier for bathroom breaks. If you ride deep snow or sit in it often, get bibs; otherwise pants work fine.</p></div>

<h2>The difference</h2>
<p>Both are waterproof outer layers for your legs. Pants stop at the waist like any trousers. Bibs extend up over your stomach and lower chest, with suspender-style straps over the shoulders, similar to overalls. Your jacket goes over the top of either one.</p>

<table>
  <thead><tr><th scope="col">Compare</th><th scope="col">Bibs</th><th scope="col">Pants</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><th scope="row">Snow protection</th><td>Best: no gap at the waist when you fall or sit</td><td>Good with a jacket powder skirt; gaps possible in deep snow</td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row">Warmth</th><td>Slightly warmer: covers your core</td><td>Cooler, better in spring</td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row">Fit</th><td>Stays up without a belt; no waistband pressure</td><td>Familiar fit; may need a belt or adjusters</td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row">Bathroom breaks</th><td>Slower: look for drop seats or side zips</td><td>Easy</td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row">Layering</th><td>Adds a layer under the jacket</td><td>More flexible</td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row">Best for</th><td>Powder, park, beginners, kids, cold days</td><td>Groomers, warm days, simplicity</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h2>Why riders love bibs</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>No snow down your back.</strong> When you fall in powder, sit to strap into a snowboard, or dig out of a tree well, bibs seal the gap between your jacket and pants.</li>
  <li><strong>They stay put.</strong> Suspenders hold them up, so there's no sagging or belt digging in, which many snowboarders appreciate when sitting and getting up all day.</li>
  <li><strong>A bit more core warmth.</strong> The extra panel over your stomach and lower back adds a layer where you lose heat.</li>
  <li><strong>Pockets.</strong> Many bibs have a chest pocket that's handy for a phone or pass.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Why some skiers stick with pants</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Bathroom breaks are easy.</strong> With bibs you usually have to take your jacket off unless they have a drop seat, side zips, or a dedicated fly.</li>
  <li><strong>Cooler in spring.</strong> On warm days, one less layer over your core helps.</li>
  <li><strong>Simple layering.</strong> Pants pair with any jacket, and many jackets have powder skirts or can zip to matching pants to block snow.</li>
</ul>

<h2>What to look for in either</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Waterproof, breathable fabric</strong> with sealed or taped seams if you ride in falling snow.</li>
  <li><strong>Shell vs insulated:</strong> a shell lets you layer underneath and works across seasons; insulated suits cold-blooded riders and very cold resorts.</li>
  <li><strong>Leg vents</strong> to dump heat on warm days or hikes.</li>
  <li><strong>Boot gaiters</strong> (inner cuffs) that seal over your boots, plus reinforced cuffs that resist edge cuts.</li>
  <li><strong>Fit with your layers:</strong> try them on over a base layer and in a squat to make sure you can move freely.</li>
</ul>
<p>Browse <%= gear_link "ski and snowboard bibs", "ski bibs" %> or <%= gear_link "insulated ski pants", "insulated ski pants" %>. For more detail, see <%= link_to "how to choose ski pants", guide_path("best-ski-pants") %> and the <%= link_to "snowboard pants guide", guide_path("snowboard-pants") %>.</p>

<h2>Which should you buy?</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Beginner snowboarder:</strong> bibs. You'll sit and fall a lot while learning.</li>
  <li><strong>Powder chaser or tree skier:</strong> bibs, especially in deep, light snow.</li>
  <li><strong>Kids:</strong> bibs keep snow out and stay up on small bodies.</li>
  <li><strong>Groomer skier or spring skier:</strong> pants are simpler and cooler.</li>
</ul>
<p>Not sure what else to wear? Here's <%= link_to "what to wear skiing", guide_path("what-to-wear-skiing") %>, layer by layer.</p>
<% faqs << { question: "Are bibs better than pants for snowboarding?", answer: "For many snowboarders, yes. Snowboarders sit down and fall more often, and bibs keep snow out of the waistband and stay up without a belt." } %>
<% faqs << { question: "Are ski bibs warmer than pants?", answer: "Slightly. Bibs cover your stomach and lower back, adding a layer over your core, but the overall warmth depends more on insulation and your base layers." } %>
<% faqs << { question: "How do you go to the bathroom in ski bibs?", answer: "Many bibs have a drop seat, side zips, or a fly so you don't have to take them all the way off. Without those features, you usually need to remove your jacket and lower the straps." } %>
